Audi: "Dieselgate" refurbishment takes longer than planned
"The diesel task force still exists," development board member Peter Mertens told the industry newspaper Automobilwoche.
Detained and retired Audi CEO Rupert Stadler had announced in December 2018 that the taskforce would be dissolved soon.
"In the first quarter of 2018, all engine/gearbox combinations are expected to be tested," he had said at the time.
Mertens now said: "At the beginning of July, we reached an important milestone in working up the diesel crisis.
We have completed the internal technical analyses of our VTDI engines.
The taskforce was founded in September 2015 following the discovery of the diesel scandal at Audi parent Volkswagen.
Mertens stressed that the carmaker had made "serious organizational changes" so that such a scandal would never occur again.
VW's supervisory board had postponed a decision on Stadler's future on Friday and plans to discuss further this Tuesday.
Investigating authorities accuse the Audi boss of having approved the sale of diesel cars with false exhaust gas values after becoming aware of the exhaust scams became known.
He knew about the manipulations or deliberately ignored them.
Bram Schot, Chief Sales Officer, currently heads the company as acting Chief Executive Officer.
Due to the changeover to the new emission and consumption test standard WLTP, there are production failures at Audi, said Schot the “Heilbronner Voice”.
In principle, he sees a falling demand for sedans such as the Audi models A6, A7 and A8, which are produced in Neckarsulm.
"The segment in itself is difficult," he said.
Schot relies on electromobility.
15,000 reservations have already been made for the e-tron, the e-tron, the first Audi electric car.
Grindel seeks re-election - Lahm should go to DFB Presidium
Mainz (dpa) - Reinhard Grindel wants to run for the office as DFB president again despite the criticism of his person after the World Cup and the Özil affair next year.
"I'm doing my job and I'm trying to make it as good as I can," Grindel said as a guest early Sunday morning at ZDF's "Current Sports Studio."
You have to always strive for re-election, that's what I'll do.
And then you have to see if the support is there.
In his own words, the head of the association would be "strange" if he did not "want to work on the future project" after the contract for the 2024 European Football Championship.
Grindel has been head of the association since April 2016 as successor to Wolfgang Niersbach, who was overthrown over the summer fairytale affair.
In the fall of 2016, he was elected for a first full term of three years.
The next vote will take place at the DFB-Bundestag on 27 September 2019.
According to Grindel, the future EM chief organizer Philipp Lahm is to participate in the DFB Presidium.
"I would be pleased if my colleagues in the Bureau agree that he will continue to advocate in this role in the Bureau in the future and then, of course, is cordially invited to make his experience available on sporting issues," Grindel said.
He did not want to take part in speculation whether Lahm could become his successor as DFB boss.
As a consequence of the affair of the photos of Mesut Ozil and Ilkay Gündogan with Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, Grindel expressed the wish that in the future a national player without a migrant background should become integration ambassador of the DFB.
The Brazilian-born Cacau and Celia Sasic are currently exercising this office, the latter having family roots in Cameroon.
"If someone without a migrant background works clearly into society and football and says, "the issue is important, we have to take care", that would also be an initiative to provide for a better basis for our clubs on the ground, where integration must work," Grindel said.
