The new playmaker Will Cummings excelled at the Oldenburgers with 25 points.
Brunswicks, where NBA star Dennis Schröder has been a majority owner since the start of the season, disappointed after a good first-quarter.
A 26:1 run of the baskets quickly ensured clear conditions.
Erdoğan opens mosque in Cologne
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan officially opened the Ditib Mosque in Cologne on Saturday.
Erdoğan had previously met during his three-day state visit with Chancellor Angela Merkel and German President Frank-Walter Steinmeier, among others.
Criticism of Germany
In his opening speech, Erdoğan called on the German government to extradite supporters of the Gülen movement living in Germany and to take action against Kurdish separatists.
We will protect our citizens - no matter where they live.
We will not let sympathizers of the PKK or Gülen movement shake up our citizens and harm German-Turkish friendship," he said.
For the opening of the Ditib Mosque, Cologne’s city centre had been partially closed.
Several thousand police officers were deployed - including snipers.
The Islam association Ditib is under criticism because it is considered an extended arm of the Turkish government.
"Global Citizen Festival": Music against poverty: stars, boo calls and frightening second
Musicians such as Janet Jackson, John Legend, Shawn Mendes and Cardi B have campaigned for greater engagement in the fight against poverty and disease at a joint concert in New York's Central Park.
"Engage yourself wherever you can," Mendes said at the festival in front of about 60,0000 spectators on the night of Sunday.
Thank you for coming here, it's a dream.
Cardi B performed in a red outfit with fringes and golden microphone.
I'm nervous, I'm sweating, but it's okay because I'm here and ready to change the world.
Actors such as Hughug Jackman and Forest Whitaker as well as representatives of governments, the United Nations and aid agencies also appeared at the multi-hour spectacle on stage.
In between, however, there was a frightening second, when many spectators thought the sound of a falling metal barrier for shots, became afraid and tried to escape.
Panic arose briefly, people ran through each other.
Seven spectators were slightly injured, said the organizer Hugh Evans.
However, New York police and musician Chris Martin calmed people off the stage.
"No one's trying to hurt anyone," Martin said.
You're all safe.
Tickets for the "Global Citizen Festival" concert were available free of charge on the Internet, just like every year.
If you wanted to get one, you had to read and disseminate texts, photos and videos on the Internet about the global fight against poverty and diseases.
For the numerous musicians and actors, there were cheers and applause from the audience - booing calls, on the other hand, for Republican Senator Jeff Flake.
Together with his Democratic colleague Chris Coons spoke on stage for more equality between men and women.
On Friday, however, Flake had advocated for the nomination of Brett Kavanaugh in the Supreme Court's Judiciary Committee and pushed many women in front of the head.
At least three women have allegations of abusive, including the attempted rape against Kavanaugh.
Flake called for an FBI investigation into the allegations, after two women, who according to their own reported sexual assaults, confronted him on the way to the committee meeting in an elevator.
"You can also come together with me at any time in an elevator," Flake said at the concert in New York.
Press freedom: Federal government defends expulsion of journalist
Government spokesman Steffen Seibert has defended the expulsion of a journalist from the press conference of Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) and Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan.
In the Chancellery, the Turkish journalist living in Hamburg had put on a T-shirt with the Turkish and German inscription "Freedom for Journalists" and had gone to the front row.
The T-shirt, had not been the problem, Seibert told Bild am Sonntag.
It was about a disturbance of the course of the press conference.
As a result of the journalist’s action, a strong unrest had arisen, which is unacceptable.
That's why he's caused personal protectors to take the man out.
Seibert said that it was not allowed to demonstrate political concerns at press conferences at the Federal Chancellery.
This applies completely regardless of whether it is a legitimate concern or not.
The rules are the rules.
Numerous journalists are in prison in Turkey.
Many of them are accused of alleged espionage activities or support or membership in banned parties or the Fethullah-Gülen movement.
Turkey's leadership blames the movement for the failed coup attempt on July 15, 2016.
Some of the accused journalists face several decades of imprisonment.
Foreign journalists aren’t safe from the Turkish authorities either: it was only at the beginning of September that Austrian journalist Max Zirngast was arrested.
Most recently, the cases of imprisoned journalists Meşale Tolu and Deniz Yücel had caused a stir.
Although they were released and were able to return to Germany, there are still trials in Turkey.
Munich: Florian David Fitz completely drunk with Ina Müller
His fans usually know him so fresh and handsome: Florian David Fitz.
You have to look twice: Yes, that’s really Florian David Fitz.
The popular actor was in Hamburg - and experienced a wild night!
"Inas Nacht", produced by the NDR, is known for humid conversations at the counter of the Hamburg harbour pub "Schellfischposten".
