The bed selfie shows a completely exhausted Florian David Fitz.
But he's not sick, he's just having a mighty hangover.
Directed to his fans, he posted some - arguably not quite serious - insights that he has now gained: "Rule one: NEVER go to Ina's night.
Rule two: never drink the black sow.
And splash.
And the round eggnog that Ina throws at half-three, also omits.
The consumption of alcohol is truly written in the 43-year-old's face.
His fans immediately posted a few tips to fight the aftermath.
They ranged from instant broth, fish buns, or - presumably most expedientially - headache tablets.
Others were simply amusing themselves about the sight of the usually attractive cinema star.
How Ina Müller survived the nocturnal drinking is not yet known.
However, since she has been moderating the TV fun at the counter since 2007, the 53-year-old seems to be significantly more proven...
Have a beer with Ina Müller.
Treysa struggles with vandalism and rubbish
The entrance to the 36-kilometre-long railway cycle path in the Rotkäppchenland in Treysa is difficult to find and litter.
Recipes against rampant vandalism are being sought, but solutions are scarcely emerging.
Shards, cigarette butts, garbage and rubbish, demolished garbage cans and now dismantled signs - for a long time, the entrance with seating to the bike path on the Haaße hill in Treysa has been presented.
For the showpiece railway cycle path in the Red Riding Hood a more than inglorious sight.
Members of the ADFC local group Schwalmstadt have also been dealing with others for some time, according to the bicycle traffic officer of the city of Ulrich Wüstenhagen.
The problem has already been raised in the Cycling Advisory Board.
So far, there is no solution.
The proposals ranged from the relocating of the seating group to regular emptying of the waste containers and cleaning of the areas at the entry point.
"I think the latter would be the imperative of the day - regardless of possible further activities," Wüstenhagen says.
Guido Hahn (ADFC) reports that the topic has also been introduced to the local advisory board.
Not only the rubbish drives cyclists and local residents - the seating group is a regular meeting place to celebrate on weekends.
Not only it is often out loud, in the case of scrumbs are also destroyed: ripped posts from the earth, garbage bins demolished, signs folded over, seating furniture smeared.
Volunteers have already cleaned up more often, says ADFC member Susanne Klippert.
However, the actions are only the proverbial drop on the hot stone.
They are annoyed that the resting place on the cycle path in Treysa is a meeting place for drinking.
"Along the entire cycle path is clearly the focal point here on Haaße-Hügel," confirms the cycle traffic commissioner, who is now also worried about the new cycling site in Ziegenhain, which will be opened today.
"If she suffers the same fate, it will be really expensive."
Another idea is video surveillance on the hill: "Possibly this sets the threshold much higher to seize on common good.
However, this would presuppose a police patrol coming by in a timely manner.
In principle, Wüstenhagen wants a development of the Haaße hill.
It needs to be made more attractive.
One could be very satisfied with the frequencing of the bike path so far.
Tourism marketing is still expandable.
Compared to others in Germany, our cycle path is very high-quality," says the ADFC members.
ADFC member Susanne Klippert assesses the situation as an extremely critical danger point in Ascheröder Straße in Treysa.
There is no turning lane onto the Haaße hills.
With busy traffic, cyclists may have to wait in the middle of the road between the two-sided traffic flow," she explains.
This is not a problem for sporty drivers, but for the elderly and especially for children.
Music Contest at the Kulturbahnhof: The "Kö von Hiltrup" and
The first call to the music contest was a success, creative and together.
To offer "D a song for Hiltrup" is the appeal of the district offensive Hiltrup, in the "Musikcontest for big and small, young and experienced musicians".
A contest "with winks in the sympathetic Hiltruper family," as Karl Franke put it.
In fact, it was soon a familial togetherness with coffee and cake from the volunteers of the Kulturbahnhof.
Seven formations competed, from the one-man band to the large ensemble: Alvin Jasic with pianist Marcel Untied, Henk Humberg with guitar, Rolf Ihmig, an Albachter living in Hiltrup, the Nine of the "Hiltruper Sternfarben", three members and three generations of the Trenkamp families, an ensemble from the Lummerland kindergarten and finally Rike Weßling and the Zwarties.
This contest, explained district mayor Joachim Schmidt, is a novelty.
We haven't experienced that in other neighborhoods.
What did the copywriters find themselves connected to?
Hiltruper See and Marktallee were particularly frequently mentioned among the villages.
Rolf Ihmig chose the avenue in his pop-like song as "Kö von Hiltrup".
The cultural station was also honored.
Again and again it was about experiencing togetherness in these places, especially Alvin Jasic.
Karl Franke revived the short breaks with short interviews with the performers or hints about her work.
Humberg learned that he had actually studied chemistry, but is now also teaching guitar.
Franke knew from the sound engineer Stefan Schemmelmann that he recently appeared in Ludwigshafen - at a major BASF event with Barclay James Harvest in the program.
And Alvin Jasic, meanwhile, has placed all sorts of things on Youtube.
"It's not about winning," Franke had said.
In fact, all participants received a gift from the Hiltrup gastronomy.
Nevertheless, there was something special for the jury's favorites - so that others can also listen to their songs.
Alvin Jasic and his pianist Marcel Untied can record an entire CD with sound engineer Schemmelmann.
Other musicians can record a song: Ihmig with his Hiltrup-Schlager, who particularly impressed Franke with his musicality, and the "Lummerland-Rudel": "a very nice performance" with ten children and two educators on guitar and cajon, Laura Kastner and Lina Tölle.
Like everyone, the children performed again for the finale - and even sounded "really good" without electronic support, as Franke said.
The jury was guided by the aspects of originality, reference to Hiltrup, catchiness of melody and recognition.
The jury included Gisbert Keller from the Westphalian School of Music, Andreas Klomfaß from the KvG-Gymnasium and Oliver Ahlers from the Wirtschaftsverbund as well as Jan Kaven, chairman of the district offensive.
The district offensive had launched the event on the occasion of its tenth anniversary.
She rated the event as a complete success.
Everyone was "very satisfied", and "the atmosphere was good" in the soon very full cultural station.
The word game: "Decrypto" in game test
The players form two teams sitting opposite each other.
For each page there is a screen, in it four cards with keywords that you - the old trick with the red foil - can only decipher when you have put them into it.
This ensures that the opposing side cannot read anything by mistake.
On the maps, for example, read from left to right: "1. Renaissance.
2 Windows.
3. Dice.
4. Feast."
These are the terms that have to encrypt from now on.
Because not the terms should be guessed here, but codes.
Alternately one player per team is the encryptor.
He gets a code card, on which maybe "3.4.2".
"Decrypto" does not recommend the terms behind the secret film, but their encryption codes.
His task in this case: in this order and in this example - he should describe the terms "cube", "feast" and "window" - in such a way that his own team recognizes the correct order and the opposing team as far as possible does not; because this wins the game if he succeeds twice in cracking the code.
The encryptor writes and reads aloud: "Throwing" for the cubes, "Loses" as a contrast to Fest; here, of course, something like "party" would also be possible and "Prague" because he has paid attention in school and knows the window collapse there.
That's how it goes round after round, team by team.
One does not know the terms of the other side - but they can rhyme better with every hint that the encryptor gives there.
As mysterious as possible, but not too turned off, the descriptions should be, otherwise you make it unnecessarily difficult for your team.
Unfortunately, the most cryptic thing about "Decrypto" is the rulebook.
The players will probably have to study it for quite a long time until they can finally play.
The explanatory video at decrypto.info on the net makes it easier.
But once you have started, you will be fascinated by how much feeling there is in this inconspicuous game.
Overall, however "Decrypto" is not quite as conclusive as predecessors and genre-primus "Codenames", which also puts an agent topic on a game of fewer words, but is much better to play in small groups.
And if it’s all about guessing and not about victory points, you can also easily invent a version for two.
Kulmbach: Man shows police Hitler salute and harasses guests in the pub
A 48-year-old man employed the Kulmbach police inspectorate on the night from Saturday to Sunday.
First he showed the Hitler salute in the upper city of a passing police patrol, later he also noticed in the upper city in a pub when he harassed other guests.
After he clearly alcoholically did not comply with the granted expulsion by the police, he had to spend the night at the police inspection Kulmbach in the detention cell.
In the sober state, he must then also answer criminally for the use of license plates of unconstitutional organizations.
Pressig plant was home to the drawers
The railway depot in Pressig was the hub in the Frankenwald for decades.
In 1991, a piece of railway history ended.
"When buffers slammed on buffers, it could be heard in the whole Pressig," recalls Georg Dinkel, who archives the history of the place and the station.
That didn't upset anyone.
That's where the brakes squeaked and clattered the wagons.
It was pure railway.
Statements that symbolize Dinkel's homet Pressig.
Nowhere in the Frankenwald was a place characterized by the railway.
Here was the starting point of the steep ramp mountain route to the north, which has its apex in Steinbach am Wald after an ascent of about 220 meters altitude.
To deal with that, train and thrust locomotives used to be needed, which had their home in Pressig.
In 1901, the Bavarian state parliament approved the construction of a railway workshop with 21 locomotive sheds, apprentice workshops, overheading line and railway mastery.
"Hearpiece was the hub, which had a diameter of 18 meters after a conversion in 1899," says Norbert Heidrich from Hirschaid, railway historian and for 47 years.
Locomotives for the secondary lines Pressig were also operated by the railway depot (BW) Pressig, which was subordinated to Bamberg in 1968, locomotives for the secondary lines Pressig - Tettau, Ludwigsstadt - Lehesten, Stockheim - Sonneberg and Kronach - Nordhalben.
On July 15, 1950, the "Volksblatt" reported a workforce of 160 men; 13 vaping locomotives and eight elloks were stationed in Pressig at that time.
The electrification of the line in 1939 already minimized the need for train and push locomotives.
From 1977 they were only needed for freight trains.
By 1991, at least 10 000 carriages per month were still ranked.
In 1982 parts of the plant were demolished, in 1991 the rest were demolished.
Heidrich: "It is unfortunate that one has not envisaged a better use of the site, also in terms of freight transport".
At Pentecost 1989, the Nuremberg Railway Directorate Celebrated a pompous station festival in Pressig for the anniversary "50 Years of Electrification Nuremberg - Saalfeld".
"There were 10 000 enthusiastic spectators here," recalls Georg Dinkel.
A little later the end.
On June 1, 1991, the last 30 men were withdrawn, the shunting yard Pressig was history.
"The shortakers and inhibitors did the most difficult work in train traffic," recalls Dinkel.
Any mistake would have had fatal consequences.
Why Pressig-Rothenkirchen station led for the reversal of the hierarchy of the two places and many more historical and up-to-date pictures can be found here in the infrankenPlus article.
