As part of the series "Art and Pleasure", a special exhibition was opened in Mittelehrenbach over the weekend.
Organizers and visitors registered the ideal weather with great relief when the special vernissage "Windspiel-Performance" was opened in the open-air area of the Dahlmann-Schmidt, Mittelehrenbach estate.
It takes place under the umbrella of the tourism association "Rund ums Walberla" as part of the series "Kunst und Genuss".
This opened its doors in the 15th year.
A large number of visitors showed the fascination of the exhibition.
21 copies of 20 artists can now be visited in Mittelehrenbach.
Owner Ernst Jürgen Dahlmann welcomed the artists to the opening of the vernissage.
He's been at it since the first hour.
Just like Volker Hahn, who made a name for himself as the main organizer and artist alike.
Surprisingly, however Hahn announced his resignation a few days ago.
With Tania Engelke and Kurt Neubauer, however, he placed his task in proven, young hands with a good future perspective.
In the past, both have actively participated in the selection of artists and co-created flyers.
On the occasion of Kunst & Genuss this year, there will once again be a 70-cent special stamp designed by them, available at every art station.
This year's poster "Betzn austanzen", designed by Volker Hahn, can also be purchased there for eight euros.
Hahn was pleased with the high popularity of the special exhibition.
Max Marcus Stadler from Nuremberg could be won for the musical framing.
When walking through the spacious garden, the visitor quickly realizes that the title "Windspiel-Performance" literally challenges the artistic imagination and ultimately expresses it playfully.
These are artworks made of metal or ceramic, often provided with color accents.
Already at the entrance of the property awaits the visitor on the right side of the "Glücksbaum" by the artist Werner Baur, Eschenau.
It is a tree stump, brightened in color, playfully equipped with 20 wind turbines, 24 horseshoes and 24 aluminum tubes as a sound piece.
With the sun flashing through the bushworks in the background, the object appears fairy-tale and invites you to lingering.
