MGV 68 Albachten: Singers celebrate their 50th birthday festively
1968 - this was the time of the protest and the student movement.
And also the Albachtians made their displeasure at that time.
At least five of them.
They did not want to come to terms with the fact that after the dissolution of the church choir "Cäcilia", there should no longer be a singing life in the district.
It has now become a legend: at a bench in the Tinnenbusch, they launched the MGV 68 Albachten.
That was 50 years ago.
Now the anniversary was celebrated large in the "House of Encounter".
"It's going to be full today," Chairman Jürgen Holstiege guessed, and he should be right.
There were 150 guests, a real success for the Albachten singers.
Especially for the anniversary, they had revived the foundation festival.
For years it was a tradition, then there was a break.
Just in time for its 50th anniversary, the traditional festival was back.
An elegant ball it became, festive clothing had been asked before.
The guests did not sit as usual in the "house of encounters" in rows of seats, but comfortably at tables.
In between, movement was announced: DJ Matthias Drees from Nottuln put on the controllers and asked on the dance floor.
It is a matter of honor that the singers of the MGV 68 Albachten themselves made the voice sound.
In keeping with the occasion, joyful songs were announced.
Almost set: "Such a day, as beautiful as today".
Matching the exquisite vine juice that many had in front of them, there was the "Rüdesheim wine".
In reference to another cool drink "Humulus lupulus", which is the Latin term for "hops".
The choral conduction of Richard Homann, who is one of the fives who were in the Tinnenbusch at the time, was sovereign.
"It's just fun to sing under his direction," Holstiege said. "He's got such an easy way, it's a masterpiece he's accomplishing." By the way, this time it wasn't just men singing.
The women's choir "Hohes C" was also a guest, whose repertoire mainly includes evergreens from the 1920s to the 1960s.
Finally, there was poetry.
Singer Theo Weber had been sitting at the desk until three o'clock in the morning to create his own rhymes.
And Andreas Winterhoff also presented well-sounding poetry.
There was also a large tombola.
With the proceeds, a visit to the all-weather zoo is to be financed for the children of the Albatros Youth Center.
