Hans von Borsody: Heide Keller never felt good enough for her husband
On the "dream ship," Heide Keller became a star.
Now she speaks openly about the fact that the series may have been complicated for her marriage-off with Hans von Borsody.
Her role of Chefhostess Beatrice von Ledebur on the "dream ship" made Heide Keller (76) famous, but on January 01, 2018 she disembarked forever.
The series, which made her a star, could be co-responsible for her failed marriage to Hans von Borsody (1929 -2013), with whom she was married for ten years.
“That could also have been a reason.
Because I grew up with this work, traveled around the world alone with a suitcase and many admired colleagues perceived me on an equal footing," Keller says in conversation with the "Bild am Sonntag".
At first, she had always been "the wife of Hans von Borsody", but that had changed with the "dream ship".
Because now the journalists only called Heide Keller.
“Whether he liked it so much, I don’t know.”
She considers it possible that her then husband might have been quite envious, but "like many men, Hans also shied away from conversations about problems and feelings."
Keller had always tried for Hans von Borsody "to be the only right one and he didn't want to get to know my fears, my weaknesses.
This means in good German: I have pretended," as she explains further.
“I’ve always had the feeling of not being good enough for him.
He saw in me an emancipated woman, which in truth I never was, even though I sometimes seemed cheeky and courageous on stage.
“I wanted our love to be forever.”
After an already failed marriage to Thomas Härtner, Keller said yes again, "although I was afraid that it could also go wrong a second time".
The day in front of the civil ceremony, I sat in the cloakroom howling and wanted to cancel everything.
Marriage to von Borsody also failed.
Why didn't it ever work out with the men?
Because I wanted too much.
At that time, Keller had imagined love “as in the film.
Unfortunately, this vision isn't suitable for everyday use.
“I’ve approached love too naively.”
