Elon Musk, founder of electric car maker Tesla, has escaped his looming dismissal as boss of the multibillion-dollar firm.
He and the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) settled an out-of-court legal battle over misleading Twitter messages in which Musk could have lost permission to run listed companies.
As part of the settlement, Musk and Tesla will pay $20 million each, and Musk will resignate for three years the office of chairman, a position comparable to Germany's supervisory board.
He is allowed to remain chief executive against it.
In the settlement, which has yet to be uphearded by a court, neither Musk nor Tesla acknowledged misconduct.
The SEC had sued the world-renowned manager in the U.S. Federal Court of Manhattan for fraud.
Musk, who also presides over the space company SpaceX, is the public face of the loss-making electric car pioneer, which is now valued at more than $50 billion on the stock market.
Musk isn't aware of any guilt
Musk explained that he did nothing wrong and was saddened and disappointed by the lawsuit.
He has always acteded in the sense of truth, transparency and investors.
On August 7, Musk had written to his more than 22 million Twitter supporters that he might take the company off the stock market and pay $420 per share, the financing was secured.
Tesla would have been valued at $72 billion overall.
Musk later rowed back: Tesla would remain on the stock market.
Following the settlement, Tesla must now find an outside-facing chairman, hire two independent board members, and also set up a committee that oversees Musk's communications.
