Polls: 46 percent don't want Merkel as chancellor by 2021
After the quarrels of the past weeks, the GroKo collects several survey low blows.
The SPD falls behind the AfD in a questioning - Angela Merkel is viewed critically.
The AfD has overtaken the SPD for the first time in the Emnid-Sunday trend for the Bild am Sonntag.
In the opinion research institute's weekly poll, the AfD rises by one point to 17 percent, the SPD drops a point compared to the previous week and lands at 16 percent.
CDU and CSU also lose a counter and only come to 27 percent.
That's the deepest value so far for the Union in Sunday's trend.
The Greens reach 15 percent, the Left 11 and the FDP 10 percent.
According to Emnid, the ruling parties SPD, CDU and CSU would only get 43 percent of the votes together.
In doing so, the grand coalition has currently lost the majority at all major research institutes - with the exception of Allensbach - on Sunday's issue.
The ruling parties consistently get 43 to 45 percent of the votes (Allensbach: 51 percent).
In the 2017 federal election, they still received a combined 53.4 percent.
For the other parties, the range is also wide: The AfD, in several polls second strongest party, comes to 15 to 18 percent.
The Greens achieve vote shares of 12.5 to 17 percent.
The left stands at an average of 10 percent, FDP at 9 percent.
For statistical reasons, depending on the share of votes, such polls have an uncertainty of 2 to 3 per cent upwards and downwards.
But support for Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) among the population has also dropped significantly since the 2017 federal election.
Currently, only 48 percent of the citizens find that Merkel is the right person in the chancellery, as the Emnid survey on behalf of the BamS revealed.
According to the poll, only one in two (50 percent) currently want the grand coalition to hold until the next regular federal election in three years, 41 percent are in favor of a new election.
Significant loss of popularity for Merkel had also been seen in a further survey published on Saturday.
Germans are also assessingingly whether Merkel should run for the CDU chairmanship again in December.
47 percent are in favor, according to Emnid, while 44 percent oppose it.
Among Union supporters, 64 percent want a candidacy, 35 percent do not.
