Allbright Foundation Report: Women Remain Raritity on Corporate Boards
Thus, only 8 percent of the boards of directors were female as of September 1, according to the report "The power of monoculture", which will be officially presented by the Allbright Foundation this Monday and was available in advance to the German Press Agency.
"The companies will have to position themselves differently, otherwise the international competitors will move past them," said foundation managing director Wiebke Ankersen.
In the USA, Great Britain or Sweden, purely male management teams - with a few exceptions - are no longer conceivable.
In comparison, the backlog makes German competitors less diverse, innovation-friendly and future-oriented.
It's a different corporate culture.
Moreover, the slight increase in the proportion of women among the German stock exchange companies was solely due to positive impulses among the companies in the index of medium-sized values (MDax).
According to Allbright, only 6.8 percent of the board of directors are female.
In the previous year, however, the figure was 3.8 percent.
Among the 30 Dax companies - pioneers in recent years - the proportion of women stagnated.
Most recently, 26 women worked on the boards.
"I'm sure the Dax companies will recruit consistent women again in the coming year - in their own interest," Ankersen said.
Only RWE and Heidelberg Cement had signaled that they would continue to make no self-commitment to increase the share of "zero percent".
A different picture emerges among the supervisory boards: As of 1 September, the proportion of women there was 30.5 percent - 45 women more and 68 fewer than a year ago.
"Recruiting just as many women annually for the boards of directors would have reached a share of 40 percent as early as 2023," the report said.
