VW's core brand is missing: Paris motor show in sign of diesel debate
Every two years, the Paris Motor Show takes place alternately with the IAA in Frankfurt.
Paris - Officially not an issue and yet ubiquitous: The diesel scandal and the struggle for driving bans and retrofits should not let go of the German car companies even at the industry meeting in Paris.
From next week, together with the competition from all over the world, they will be showcasing their novelties in the French capital at the 2018 Motor Show.
Diesel no longer plays a major role.
However, the possible exchange and retrofitting offers for diesel owners in Germany, which are still to be negotiated with the government in Berlin by then, are likely to be an important topic, at least behind the glossy scenes of the exhibition stands.
A big name is missing in Paris: The Volkswagen Group is involved with Audi, Porsche and other subsidiaries, but not with the core brand VW itself.
Audi and Mercedes-Benz are here and, among other things, have their first pure electric models with them: the e-tron of the Ingolstadt-based company is to be launched at the end of the year, the EQC of Stuttgart 2019.
However, both have already been officially presented.
BMW's iNext won't start until 2021.
At the heart of Paris is the new 3 Series.
In total, more than 250 exhibitors will be represented this year from 4 to 14 October.
In addition to car companies, motorcycle builders as well as numerous mobility service and software providers are also present in their own halls.
The fair, which celebrates its 120th birthday this year, takes place every two years alternately with the IAA in Frankfurt.
More than a million visitors arrived in 2016.
At least 380 people have died in the major earthquakes and subsequent tsunami on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi.
Dozens are still missing.
In addition, there are more than 500 injured.
The island was rocked by two earthquakes on Friday.
The first quake had 5.9 magnitude, the second the magnitude 7.4.
Communication and transport links are damaged, resulting in enormous property damage.
After the quakes, the tsunami broke over the west coast of Sulawesi.
Hundreds had gathered on the beach in the coastal town of Palu for a feast.
It is feared that the casualty figures are still skyrocketing significantly.
So far, the figures come only from Palu.
However, numerous other communities are also affected - along Sulawesi's west coast.
