Geisingen: Geisinger Christians celebrate Holy Cross festival
The Geising Catholics celebrate the Holy Cross festival on September 14 or on Sunday after that.
The festive service takes place in the municipal church, followed by a procession to the Holy Cross Church with the closing blessing.
The Holy Cross Church houses the shot Christ, who dates from the 30-Year War and is said to have been fired by a Swedish soldier, according to legend.
Blood flowed out of the gunshot wound.
From this a great pilgrimage developed to the shot Christ, which then also led to the construction of the Holy Cross Church.
This is not finished to this day, only the altar space stands, the missing longhouse is indicated by the trees.
In his sermon, Pastor Adolf Buhl recalls that the cross is a sign of hope, consolation and life.
The service was poorly attended despite the importance for the Geising parish.
After the service, the procession to the Church of the Holy Cross took place and with the closing blessing, the ecclesiastical part of the Cross-Festion ended.
For many years, the Catholic church choir invites to the secular part of the festival in the Catholic parish.
The choir members provided the visitors with hearty dishes as well as a large selection of cakes and adapted to the weather, farm ice cream.
In the afternoon, the parish still offered guided tours around the municipal church, Bernd Bendschneider and Petra Schmid-Seger informed the visitors on behalf of the parish about the damage to the façade, roof, the sandstone works and windows as well as the upcoming construction measures, which will drag on until next year.
Since the scaffolding has been standing for some time, the damage could be determined more precisely and the individual tenders could be prepared more concretely.
What is certain is that the renovation is more expensive than planned, because the damage already existing a few years ago has become larger, but more have also been determined, which has only been made possible by inspection by means of scaffolding.
The plastering work has been completed, the natural stone work is still to be started before the winter and one hopes to complete a good half of this work this year.
Inauguration: New hall of residence in Neu-Ulmer Münsterblickstraße
Lebenshilfe Donau-Iller opens a new dormitory for people with disabilities in Münsterblickstraße.
That "a very special house" is now handed over to his destination in Münsterblickstraße - all those present agreed at the inauguration date.
After all, the twelve wheelchair users, who have been living for some time in the dormitory of Lebenshilfe Donau-Iller, are dependent on the special architecture of the new building.
Larger corridors, corridors and doors, larger rooms and barrier-free access for all areas architect Rudolf Bader has taken into account in his concept.
The first planning began about eight years ago, when the former officer building of the U.S. Army was put to the test for the residents with mobile phone cap: "The house had gotten old and no longer contemporary in the equipment," Bader explained in retrospect.
In 2016, the excavators arrived to demolish the house.
The new building should be designed as friendly and bright as possible so that the inhabitants can feel comfortable in it, according to the architect.
Lebenshilfe Donau-Iller opens new dormitory
Lebenshilfe invested around €4.6 million in the new building.
The Free State contributed 2.4 million euros, the district of Swabia 450000 euros.
According to architect Bader, the construction work has remained within the planned framework - both in terms of time and financially.
He was also thanked by the managing director of Lebenshilfe, Jürgen Heinz.
The balancing act between a barrier-free and at the same time fire-proof design had not always been easy.
Nevertheless, the planners and craftsmen with the many stipulations had built a home that allows the residents to experience the freedom to move even without outside help.
Gertrud Kreutmayer, head of the social administration of the district of Swabia, also commented similarly: "The house is based on the special needs of its residents, who need aids to be mobile".
The comfort and functionality had been well combined, Kreutmayer said.
"The property in this location is a real stroke of luck, because in today's situation it can no longer be acquired".
In total, the new dormitory in Münsterblickstraße offers space on almost 1500 square meters for 30 people with disabilities, which are divided into three residential groups.
Lebenshilfe managing director Heinz is proud of the eleven full-time employees, all of whom are employed according to tariff.
Germany: Schwesig laments differences between East and West
Ahead of Unity Day, Deputy SPD Federal Chairman Manuela Schwesig appealed to all compatriots not to come to terms with the discrimination of East Germans.
There are differences that hurt after almost 30 years of unity.
We must not be satisfied with these differences," the Prime Minister of Mecklenburg-Vorpommern told the Tagesspiegel am Sonntag newspaper.
The goal must remain to create equal living conditions across the country.
It is unacceptable that East Germans worked on average longer than the people in West Germany, but get 15 percent less salary.
"I can't explain that to anyone almost 30 years after German unity," Schwesig said.
North Korea: Will this cute dog end the Korean conflict?
One-year-old Pungsan hunting dog is said to be smoothinging the waves between the two Koreas.
In an effort to ease relations with Seoul, Pyongyang has gifted two North Korean Pungsan hunting dogs to South Korean President Moon Jae.
As Moon's office announced on Sunday, the two approximately one-year-old dogs reached their recipient as early as Thursday.
They passed the border town of Panmunjom together with three kilograms of dog food in order to "facilitate" the animals "facilitation".
The dogs with the thick white fur, pointed ears and brown eyes are considered loyal and intelligent.
They belong to the national cultural property of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea.
The gift of friendship had been announced by the North Korean leader Kim Jong Un at the third summit this year in Pyongyang in mid-September.
Both countries also agreed to compete together in international sports competitions such as the 2020 Olympic Games and apply together to host the Olympic Games in 2032.
In the course of the mutual rapprochement, Kim also agreed to close the Tongchang Ri test facility for rocket propulsion.
