Wolfgang Maderthaner, historian and director-general of the Austrian State Archives, puts it quite differently in the magnificent "Bilderbuch" Austria, which will be published on 1 October.
New Week, New Opportunity for Government
Back to technical work - that's the unofficial motto for today's working week of the grand coalition.
Hardly an interview, hardly a speech in which the chancellor and her ministers from the CDU, SPD and also from the CSU did not summon the "return to factual work" mantra-like.
This may have led many craftsmen, doctors, cashiers and other working citizens in this country to the legitimate question of what is actually the difference between work and technical work, but be it.
If the federal government is now ready to devote itself again to its mandate - governing - it should not be stopped now with conceptual debates.
After the provocations and embarrassments of the last few weeks, Union and SPD can actually still achieve something together, will probably already show Monday.
In the evening, the leaders of the ruling parties come together for the coalition committee.
Chancellor Angela Merkel now wants to convene this round regularly to defuse conflicts early; a lesson from all the dispute.
Initially, however, conflict-ridden issues are back on the agenda with the diesel retrofittings and the Skilled Immigration Act.
CSU Transport Minister Scheuer wants to let the car companies get away with exchange premiums, SPD and CDU see the manufacturers in the obligation to technically retrofit older diesels.
While a rapprochement has emerged in the diesel crisis over the weekend, Union and SPD are still far apart in the planned skilled labour immigration law.
The SPD calls for a prospect of staying for rejected asylum seekers who are working or doing an education.
The Union is strongly opposed to the "change of lanes" from the asylum system into the labour market.
So there will be disputes again among the coalition partners - and how should it be otherwise?
Not equal and equal has joined forces for GroKo, but three partners with sometimes very different societal images and priorities.
Arguing is good.
Without a dispute, a society drawn by so diverse, sometimes contradictory interests does not progress.
However, in order to be constructive, disputes must not again be inflated for party-tactical reasons on the question of being or non-being.
It's still about politics, not theatre.
Union and SPD argue - but always have the reality of life of the people in mind.
What is feasible and good for many?
With this guiding question, both the diesel and lane change questions can be pragmatically clarified - and thus regain trust.
Above all, this is why the popular parties now have to fight.
