Police tear up a road in search of women's body
Marxgrün - The police did not want to comment on this for a long time.
There's a confirmation now.
Early Saturday morning has been dug on the state road between Naila and Bad Steben.
The track is still closed until Monday in the Marxgrün area.
This was preceded by the fact that the investigators had previously used an archaeology dog.
The dog specially trained for the smell of old human bones, together with its guide, was finally able to confine the relevant place to a few meters in several operations.
In addition, together with scientists from the Technical University of Munich and the top Bavarian monument protection authority, a soil anomaly was discovered in the area of the said street.
For more than a year, prosecutors and criminal police have been processing the missing persons case.
Again and again at Marxgrün a bike path was closed and examined with sonar devices, because the investigators suspect that Heike's body may have been buried here in the course of the new construction of the state road 30 years ago.
Color powders and eggs: riots in Barcelona ahead of Catalonia anniversary
On Monday marks the first anniversary of the controversial independence referendum in Catalonia.
But there are riots even before that.
The separatists have not forgotten the police operation of autumn 2017.
Shortly before the anniversary of the Catalan independence referendum on 1 October 2017, there have been clashes between separatists and the police in central Barcelona.
Catalan security forces were attacked by the independence supporters, among other things, with color powder when they wanted to prevent them from approaching a demonstration by the National Police and the Civil Guard.
Around 3000 officers from the Policía Nacional and Guardia Civil police units wanted to honore their colleagues with a rally, who were on duty at polling stations last year in the referendum.
In an attempt to prevent citizens from voting, there had also been violence at that time.
Catalan police would have formed a chain to keep the demonstrations of the separatists and central state security forces separate, Spanish media reported.
However, some of the approximately 6000 separatists tried by force to break through the police chain, and pelted the officers, among other things, with paint bags and eggs.
Six people had been arrested and 24 others were slightly injured, it said, citing the authorities.
Monday marks the first anniversary of the independence referendum.
After the vote and a subsequent decision to secede the prosperous region from Spain, the central government in Madrid had placed Catalonia under compulsory administration in the autumn of 2017.
The then regional president Carles Puigdemont fled into exile in Belgium.
Other politicians went down abroad as well.
On the other hand, several ministers of the then regional government and other activists of the separatist movement are still in custody.
In December, new elections were held in the region.
After taking office of the new Catalan government in May, the coercived administration was terminated.
But the new regional president Quim Torra also insists on the "right of self-determination" of Catalans.
