Smears with local political message: No successful advertising for skating facility
In Lengerich, strangers have campaigned for the construction of a skate facility in a hardly conducive way.
In several places there are corresponding smears, says Jürgen Kohne from the city administration at the request of the WN.
In at least one case, criminal charges have been lodged with the police for damage to property.
The three words "Skatepark für Lengerich" were displayed in large letters on signs, among others at the Gempt-Halle and at the Old Town Hall.
Michael Rottmann, manager of the hall, reports that a corresponding lettering had already been discovered on the advertising sign at the staircase to the Gempt-Bistro after the professional election fair on 19 September.
Since the short message at that time had apparently "only" been left with an edding, it could easily be removed.
"A few days later, however, it stood there again, this time sprayed on," says Rottmann.
He assumes that the corresponding advertising sign must be replaced.
A special note gets this case because several large companies from Lengerich are also mentioned on the board.
If the skate facility is built, its realization should also be made possible by sponsors.
There are already commitments, explains Jörg Hesselmann from the municipal department of school, sports and culture, without mentioning names.
It is quite possible, however, that one or more of the sponsors are identical to those companies that stand on the smeared board.
Local politics and administration have been dealing with the topic of skating for years.
After the old one had been dismantled at the LWL-Klinik, the search for an alternative location began after some time.
Finally, the council voted in favour of the firefighting parking lot.
However, this request was not implemented - too many factors apparently spoke against a construction at this point.
At the end of June, a U-turn followed.
The members of the Main and Finance Committee now voted unanimously in favour of the Gutenberg School area.
Jürgen Kohne says about the state of affairs that the administration is currently in the middle of concrete planning.
The last word has the city council.
He assumes a decision this year, so could be built with a positive vote in 2019.
Air traffic control bottleneck gets even worse
Frankfurt/Hamburg After the European aviation chaos in the summer, the German air traffic controllers see no signs of an improvement in the coming year.
"This year we had wind, in 2019 we will have strong wind and then comes the storm," Matthias Maas, the head of the air traffic control union, told the German Press Agency ahead of the aviation summit on October 5 in Hamburg.
An important reason for the numerous delays and flight cancellations is the long-known shortage of staff among European air traffic controllers, for which there is no quick solution.
"The airlines and the airports can achieve rapid improvements for the coming year, but the training of new controllers lasts four to five years," the unionist said.
Europe-wide, the market for ready-to-educated pilots is empty.
Around a hundred people were missing in the centre of Karlsruhe alone to monitor the upper German airspace.
Maas again criticized that various aviation trade unions were not invited to the summit.
Representatives of the federal government, states, air traffic control, airlines and airports will meet in the Hanseatic city on Friday to address the causes of the delays.
"If you want to talk about additional work densification and overtime, this will not be possible without us," explained the head of the GdF, which has many members at Germany's German air traffic control.
Maas again blamed specifications of the EU Commission for the pilot misery.
The traffic forecasts for the regulatory period, which runs until 2019, were far too low.
The resulting cost targets and personnel savings for national air traffic control systems are now fully impacted on the operational business.
Maas appealed to Federal Transport Minister Andreas Scheuer (CSU) to campaign in Brussels for regulations that "do not cut off the air even further" for air traffic control.
Currently, the EU requirements would be coordinated by 2024 and further tightening is to be feared.
The EU is subjected to constant lobbying pressure from airlines.
"Ryanair CEO Michael O'Leary and his colleagues are now reaping exactly what they've sown themselves in recent years," Maas said.
He hoped that the high cost of delay at the airlines would rethink.
The GdF will only participate in the improvement of the situation if the German air traffic control (DFS) offers a "harmonious training and future pact," Maas said.
The previously announced admission of 120 pilot students annually over the next five years is just enough to compensate for the age-related departures.
The necessary staff build-up is not yet associated with it.
Thyssenkrupp supervisory board votes for group restructuring
The members of the Thyssenkrupp Supervisory Board voted unanimously in favour on the plans to restructure the Group.
According to Thyssenkrupp interim CEO Guido Kerkhoff, who was appointed chairman of the board at Sunday's meeting, the crisis-hitting group is to be split into two self-employed companies.
Both companies are said to be based in Essen and listed on the stock exchange.
There should be no additional staff reduction beyond the already known cuts in the administration.
After two resignations, the control panel on the capital side counts only eight heads.
This is confronted by ten representatives of the employees.
However, the final decision is expected to be made by an Annual
Among other things, the new Thyssenkrupp Materials AG is expected to include the 50 percent share from the merged steel business with Indian partner Tata.
Thyssenkrupp Industrials AG is intended to bundle the business of elevators and subsidies in the second company Thyssenkrupp Industrials AG.
Barcelona: Violence at demos of police officers and Catalan independence advocates.
At two simultaneous demonstrations by Spanish police and Catalan independence supporters in Barcelona, there have been violent clashes with numerous casualties.
According to the city administration, about 3000 police officers from all over Spain gathered on Saturday as about 6000 independence supporters also demonstrated.
The Catalan police had tried with a large contingent to keep the two protest trains away from each other.
According to the emergency forces, however, independence advocates repeatedly tried to break through the security barrier.
They would have attacked the officers with paint and throwing bullets.
According to the information, there were also between the participants of the various protest marches.
According to rescue workers, 24 people were injured.
The police said there had been six arrests.
Better salaries and appreciation of colleagues
With their demonstration, the police had wanted to pay tribute to the colleagues who had acteded almost exactly a year agove against the independence referendum banned by the Spanish central government in Catalonia.
At the same time, they demanded better salaries.
Relatives of the Spanish police and the Guardia Civil find it unfair that Catalan and Basque police officers deserve significantly more than them.
The protesters found it a provocation that the Spanish policers demonstrated in Barcelona.
"Why did they come here to protest?" 55-year-old Catalan protester Manel Perez asked AFP.
Only to provoke and celebrate the fact that a year agove.
A 31-year-old police officer from the Balearic Islands, who only wanted to give his name with Daniel, dismissed the allegation.
"Of course we want to support our comrades, who last year only did their duty: defend the law," he said.
But the demonstration is there to enforce our rights and we're doing it in Barcelona because that's a big European city and there's more echo here.
However, he and his colleagues "did not come to provoke," the policeman assured.
