Music cabaret: Gender understanding with heart - Wolbeck - Westphalian News
"Both suitable for adults and men" was the musical cabaret with Tina Teubner on Friday evening in the hall of the Christ Church in Wolbeck.
As a cabaret, Teubner buttoned up the gender understanding, not only with humour and heart’s warmth for the (feeled) woman and (felt) man and whatever else there may be, but also with music.
She used the mini-guitar and mastered her violin.
Ben Süverkrüp sat on the wing.
On the one hand, this is the male sparring partner, who sometimes also speaks until the end of the sentence, and, on the other hand, a true pianist: Süverkrüp Teubner is so congenially harmonically accompanied that some may have wished for a solo.
This also existed, with Beethoven and Chopin, but differently, namely the way it matched the red thread of the evening.
Teubner had brought a paradox into play: The satisfaction of the man comes if he had a partner, but she does not open the door.
A closed door without a partner, on the other hand, brings no benefit.
The piano solo now demonstrates the same: What would have sprung from composinging Mozart had "das Nannerl" opened the door unexpectedly?
Süverkrüp glides across improvisations, from Bach's "O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden" into the "Song of Death," and from Mozart's G minor symphonie into a playful dance.
There was great applause and "Bravo" from the full hall.
Karlheinz Kleintje, treasurer of the association, handered flowers to the chansonette.
But Chairman Heidemarie Markenbeck didn’t take an additional thanks.
She had the idea, in addition to performances in Stuttgart and Frankfurt, to treat the duo to one in Wolbeck.
Keywords of the article "Gender understanding with heart"
