From pure fruit cultivation to ecology
With a gala in the Dr.-Stammberger-Halle, the 125th anniversary of the district association for horticulture and state care was celebrated.
On September 29, 1893, the last founding impulses for the Kulmbach district association for horticulture and state nursing were set with a fruit show in the Kulmbacher Vereinshaus, and exactly 125 years later he celebrated a big anniversary on Saturday evening in the Kulmbacher Stadthalle.
Of course, it was still called differently at that time: As "district orchard association Kulmbach Stadt und Land" it was launched by Mayor Wilhelm Flessa in 1893.
The purpose of the Obstbauverein also differed from today's work - it was actually purely about fruit growing, about flowers and horticulture at that time no one thought.
"At that time, in the epoch of the Enlightenment, the fruit tree was a favorite child of the elites," explained Günter Dippold in his lecture.
Fruit trees were supposed to line highways, so-called industrial gardens were created in schools.
Fruit cultivation is still the responsibility of 55 horticultural associations in the Kulmbach district, which comprise almost 7000 members.
However, the spectrum is far from exhausted.
Natural horticulture, organic plant protection, village beautification and nutrition play an equally important role as teaching knowledge, a healthy diet and youth work.
The members of the Hutschdorfer youth group "Maulwurfe" showed how versatile the tasks of a horticultural association are entertainingly on stage.
"Youth work is urgently needed, especially in the digital age," said Günter Reif, chairman of the district council, and OB Henry Schramm also emphasized the importance of experiences in nature for life.
Chestnut collecting was a huge thing for us children, I doubt that you can collect a similar amount of joy with Smartphone & Co.
Schramm promised to plant two trees together with District Administrator Klaus Peter Söllner for the anniversary.
"Where they should stand, the district association decides," added Landrat Söllner and emphasized the special importance of the horticultural associations for the villages.
The district association stands for ecology, beauty of the garden and green areas in the village.
That this is a real concern of the many horticultural associations in the district, also district managing director and specialist consultant Friedhelm Haun left no doubt.
He led the many visitors through the long history of the district association with a picture lecture and reported, among other things, the need for self-sufficiency after the war.
In the past, everything came from the garden, for the winter it was dosted.
At the end of the 1950s, however, the utility garden turned into an ornamental and residential garden.
"Nature endures, it was already there before us and we have to orientate ourselves on it," pleaded the expert for fruit and walnut trees.
Therefore, there is the incentive for the horticultural associations not to let up, so that our homeland remains beautiful and livable.
This also includes greeting the new citizens in our associations, added Wolfram Vaitl, president of the Bavarian regional association.
"We have to stop the blatant decline of insects, we owe it to our children and grandchildren," Günter Reif concluded the successful ceremony, which was solemnly frameded by the tower brass of the Thurnau music association.
Daniel Huber won summer GP in Hinzenbach
The Salzburger won with 245.7 points well ahead of Peier and Geiger.
Daniel Huber ensured a home win on Sunday at the summer Grand Prix of ski jumpers in Hinzenbach.
The Salzburger won with 245.7 points well ahead of Switzerland's Kilian Peier (237.7) and Germany's Karl Geiger (237.3).
World Cup record winner Gregor Schlierenzauer (236.2) narrowly finished fifth behind Russian overall leader Yevgeny Klimov (236.3).
Clemens Aigner (7th) and Michael Hayböck (9th) also got into the top ten.
Double world champion Stefan Kraft landed in 17th place.
In the overall standings, Huber is fifth ahead of Wednesday's final competition in Klingenthal.
Ski jumping results Sommer-Grand-Prix in Hinzenbach (OÖ) from Sunday: 1. Daniel Huber (AUT) 245.7 points (88.0 m/89.0 m) - 2. Kilian Peier (SUI) 237.7 (91.0/86.5) - 3. Karl Geiger (GER) 237.3 (87.0/88.0) - 4.
Total score after 9 out of 10 competitions: 1. Klimow 555 - 2. Geiger 416 - 3. Piotr Zyla (POL) 382 - 4. Kamil Stoch (POL) 376 - 5. Huber 305
Priority on Nafta talks: Canada's foreign minister postpones U.N. speech
Mexico's government wants clarity on Nafta: They give both trading partners an ultimatum for an agreement.
Canada's secretary of state is now giving absolute priority to the trade deal - and postponing her speech to the United Nations.
Due to the time pressure in the fight for a reissue of the North American free trade agreement (Nafta) with the U.S. and Mexico, Canadian Foreign Minister Chrystia Freeland has postponed her speech at the U.N. General Debate in New York.
Instead of this Saturday, the minister will not speak until Monday before the General Assembly of the United Nations, said her spokesman Adam Austen.
In the meantime, she is focused on renegotiating Nafta.
Mexican Economy Minister Ildefonso Guajardo said on Friday that the negotiators of Canada and the U.S. would have 48 hours to agree if Canada actually wanted to accede to the trade agreemon already made between his country and the U.S.
In August, the United States and Mexico agreed on a rewriting of the Nafta regional free trade agreement.
U.S. President Donald Trump hinted that Canada could be barred from Nafta in the future.
Discussions have been going on for over a year.
By Sunday evening (local time), they should now be brought to a conclusion, Trump's government wants to submit the agreement to the U.S. Senate before October 1.
In the event of a failure of the negotiations, Mexico's President-designate Andres Manuel López Obrador had announced on Friday to seek a bilateral agreement with Canada.
