Protests against presidential candidates Bolsonaro
Brazilian presidential candidate Jair Bolsonaro, who was injured in a knife attack, has left the hospital.
The far-right ex-military wanted to fly to Río de Janeiro, where he lives, according to a report by the daily newspaper "O Globo."
After his dismissal, it is still unclear whether the 63-year-old will make public appearances in the last week before the presidential elections in the South American country.
The politician had been attacked on the open road in early September during a campaign event in southern Brazil.
Thousands of people took to the streets in several Brazilian cities to demonstrate against Bolsonaro.
Under the title "Ele não" (in German: "Der nicht"), women's associations in particular had called for protests against the ex-military.
Also on the social networks, the associations against Bolsonaro's racist and misclassifying statements mobilized.
Several thousand protesters were expected in the economic metropolis of S�ão Paulo.
In the capital Brasilia, the protests mingled with a rally of the presidential candidate of the Brazilian Workers Party (PT), Fernando Haddad.
He replaced former President Inácio Lula da Silva, who had been sentenced to twelve years imprisoned for corruption and is therefore not allowed to run.
Brazil's presidential election is on October 7.
As ex-mayor of the city of São Paulo, Haddad has been far behind the far-right candidate Bolsonaro in polls so far.
The "Trump of Brazil" has been involved in politics for a long time, but has recently presented itself as an anti-system candidate.
If none of the candidates reaches the absolute majority in the first round of voting, there will be a run-off election on 28 October.
Observers assume Bolsonaro will not successfully emerge from this one - no matter who he competes against.
Bolsonaro is known for inciting homosexuals and black people and glorifying the military dictatorship (1964-1985).
Again and again he shocks with verbal derailings.
Another time, he said supporters of Lula's left-wing Workers' Party should be shot.
Europe reclaises Ryder Cup title
Saint-Quentin-en-Yvelines - Just an hour away from Disneyland, European golfers have written another Ryder Cup fairy tale at the gates of Paris.
Captain Thomas Björn's outsider team dethroned in front of more than 150,000 spectators on the three days at Le Golf National with great punches and outstanding team spirit the favored defending champion USA around a toothless Tiger Woods.
After long illness: Danish rock legend died
Danish rock icon Kim Larsen has died.
The singer died on Sunday at the age of 72 after a long illness, his producer Jörn Jeppesen said on Larsen's website.
Larsen was surrounded by his wife and six children on his deathbed.
The musician had been undergoing treatment for prostate cancer since the beginning of the year.
Larsen had become well-known with the band Gasolin', which was enormously successful in Scandinavia in the 1970s.
Their songs were influenced by international music greats such as Jimi Hendrix, Bob Dylan and the Beatles.
After the dissolution of the band in 1978, Larsen was successful as a solo artist.
He sold around three million records in Denmark.
His album "Midt om natten" from 1983 is the best-selling album in Denmark to date.
Danish Prime Minister Lars Lökke Rasmussen paid tribute to Larsen on Sunday in a statement as a "street boy" who was "against everything, but in everyone's hearts."
The head of government also recalled that monarchy opponent Larsen had appeared in 2010 on the 70th birthday of Queen Margrethe II.
Rock night in the shoe factory: volume at compressed air hammer level
"Here we stand, take the bone saw and ignite a concert evening with the power of a tiger". Only the band names of "Here We Stand", "Tiger Bonesaw" and "The Ignition" suggest such a description of the rocking live music experience on Saturday evening in the hall hall of the Bürgerzentrum Schuhfabrik: pure hard rock from three bands.
After the sound check and shortly before the start of the concert, the musicians even take time for a group photo.
There's no trace of stage fright.
Afterwards, the rock protagonists from Ahlen and the surrounding area - all in conjunction with the booking agency "Mosquito" from Oelde - with their bands loudly inspire the approximately 120 visitors of the "Mint Mosquito" rock night.
Already the quartet "Tiger Bonesaw" with frontman and guitarist Dennis Hadrika knows how to convince at the start of the concert evening with the latest, unknown pieces from their own pen and the soon-to-be-release CD.
In front of the stage, the band’s harmonious and punchy interplay is danced.
Although "Encorement" shouts cannot be overheard, "Tiger Bonesaw" then leave the stage to the trio "Here We Stand".
Guitarist Sascha Bregenhorn, Golo Meierhenrich on bass and drummer Kai Hölscher with their alternative sounds, sometimes reminiscent of classical rock of the 1970s, demonstrate that hard rock is not the same.
After the "High Energy Rock" of the "Tigers", "Here We Stand" also uses a synthesizer in addition to guitar rock.
Not only the longing, almost ballading "Take Me Home" impresses with the change from melodic passages to the guitar-heavy rock riffs of the multi-layered songs.
The quartet "The Ignition" from Tönnishäuschen, meanwhile, has gained a special reputation since its foundation in 2005.
Full of agility, the band presents their hard rock on Saturday.
The fact that "The Ignition" has special stage experience with its performances in front of a very large audience - recently documented in a film by Max Meis about the tour in China of 2017 - is obvious and unmistakable.
Singer and guitarist Tim Jungmann, in combination with the band, also sets his own accents in the only covered song of the evening, "Word Up" by Cameo from the 80s.
Guitarist Norbert Brinkmann delivers an acclaimed solo during the even higher sound than before.
Due to the genre, a certain volume is of course part of such a hard rock concert: According to the "Mint Mosquito" rock night, according to information of the mixing console team at the three-person concert of "Here We Stand", "Tiger Bonesaw" and "The Ignition" is about 98 decibels.
This is, for example, at compressed air hammer level, but is thus still well below the controls of the "Wacken-Fesitval", which "The Ignition" refers to on their website.
Deafening 120 decibels in jet shoot loudness, as is customary in the case of the annual metal dike in the north of the Republic, the Ahlen audience does not need at this rock night.
After almost four hours, the three bands of the harder, rocky gait have long since convinced with their sovereign, virtuoso playing and their own compositions.
"Here we stand, have taken the bone saw and ignited a concert evening with the power of a tiger," all three groups, of course figuratively, can say at the end.
And above all enjoy the great applause of the visitors.
