Trump slams Kim: 'And then we fell in love'
It is not a secret that the U.S. President of North Korea’s leader Kim is quite benevolent despite the nuclear crisis.
Now, however, Trump has made a veritable declaration of love to his colleague.
It also has to do with Kim's "beautiful letters".
At the tender age of 72, the married U.S. president has confessed to a new flame.
The tragic: it's a long-distance relationship.
In public, Donald Trump on Saturday made an unusual declaration of love to North Korean leader Kim Jong Un.
In front of supporters in the U.S. state of West Virginia, Trump founded almost tender words for the half-and-old scion of the ruling dynasty from Pyongyang, although it proves to be quite tough chunks in the struggle for North Korea's nuclear disarmament.
"I've been tough, he too, and between us it's going back and forth," Trump reported.
And then we fell in love.
All right?
No, really.
And it's not just the conversations with Kim that would have melted his heart.
He wrote me beautiful letters, they were great letters.
Their personal connection has also borne political fruit: Trump referred to Kim’s announced stop of all nuclear and missile tests, even if critics have so far seen little more than lip service.
That the affection for the "Rocket Man," notorious "Rocket Man" - according to Trump's nickname for Kim - shouldn't be secret to everyone.
In any case, he anticipated the expected criticism at his appearance in a large sports hall in the city of Wheeling: "Donald Trump says they fell in love".
How terrible, how terrible is that?
It's so unpresidential.
However, he cares very little about this, Trump explained and then directed his index finger at the crowd: "I've always told you, it's so easy to be presidential".
But then it wouldn't push 10,000 people out there into this bulging arena, but we'd only have 200 people standing outside.
