Refugees rescued from Libyan prison thanks to video
Nigerian refugees have pointed out their catastrophic detention conditions in Libya with the help of a mobile phone video, thus enabling their rescue.
The group had filmed recording in their cell in the coastal town of Sawija in July at risk of death and disseminated it online.
"They refuse to send us back," one man said in the video message.
We're suffering here, we're dying here.
The recording was sent to the "France 24 Observers" website, whose editors alerted the International Organization for Migration (IOM).
The refugees were eventually flowned out to Nigeria at the end of August.
"Without the video, we probably couldn't have returned to Nigeria," Efe Onyeka, who recorded the video, told AFP.
The 25-year-old had been arrested in Libya while attempting to cross the Mediterranean Sea.
With the video, he put himself in danger, according to his own statement.
One of his associates had been beaten by the guards almost to death, Onyeka said.
In general, the detention conditions were catastrophic: "They beat us all with pipes and sticks."
They didn't give us anything to eat, we had to drink from the latrine
The IOM organizes the voluntary repatriation of refugees stranded in Libya.
Some 2,700 migrants from Nigeria have been sented back to their homeland this year with the help of the programme, said the IOM spokesman in the West African country, Jorge Galindo.
The case of the now rescued Nigerians is not extraordinary with regard to the poor conditions of detention in Libya.
However, he has not yet heard of a call for help via mobile phone video.
International organizations have long criticized the conditions in the Libyan refugee camps as inhumane.
The North African state, largely dominated by militias, is the main transit country for migrants en route from Africa to Europe.
When a female teacher went to elementary school place on Sunday afternoon to prepare her classes for Monday, she surprised a burglar.
Around 4:50 p.m., the teacher entered the building via the main entrance.
In the forehouse of the 1st floor, she saw a man fleeing immediately.
First through a door, then over the roof terrace and from there via a staircase into the ground floor or the forecourt.
The teacher informed the police, who started the investigation.
Whether things or money were stolen cannot yet be said.
Offender description: about 16 years old, black, very short hair, slightly darker appearance, dark pants, dark T-shirt.
