German was trapped in his own car for days
A 75-year-old had to wait several days and nights in his car in a wooded area in central Franconia, after he stuck the car away from all forest roads and a tree blocked the car door.
Rescue workers accidentally spotted the car on Saturday afternoon not far from pastures and freed the missing since Tuesday evening, police said on Sunday.
The 75-year-old from Pleinfeld was in good health despite the cold nights and the long time without care.
He said he was looking forward to his home.
The pensioner had been reported missing by his family after failing to return from a ride with his SUV across the local meadows and fields on Tuesday.
Despite intensive searches, the man's whereabouts had remained unclear for days.
Laschet urges rule of law in Turkey
Cologne NRW Prime Minister Armin Laschet (CDU) has urged the rule of law in Turkey in Turkey in conversation with Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan.
The relations of the two countries are currently "overshadowed," Laschet said on Saturday after a meeting with Erdogan at Cologne's airport.
Above all, this concerns waves of arrests, freedom of the press and the freedom of religion.
"I have therefore made it clear with Erdogan that if relations are to normalize in the future, if economic relations are to be deepened, the rule of law is a very important prerequisite."
Laschet also addressed the cases of Germans who are in prison in Turkey.
He expressed the expectation that freedom of expression would be respected.
Erdogan emphasized the independence of the Turkish judiciary.
Laschet and Erdogan's conversation had been moved at short notice to a building of flight readiness by the Federal Ministry of Defence at Cologne's airport.
Originally, it was supposed to take place at Wahn Castle - but the castle owners had rejected this out of political conviction against Erdogan.
Erdogan traveled after the conversation to the opening of the Ditib Mosque in Cologne.
Laschet had canceled a joint appearance in the building.
But he said, "The Muslims who go to this mosque are also citizens of our country."
And we are also their point of contact - and not the Turkish president.
Lack of confidence in the US: North Korad makes calls for disarmament
While U.S. President Trump doesn't get tired of emphasizing the successes in the negotiations on North Korea's denuclearization, the East Asian regime portrays conditions more soberly.
Foreign Minister Ri calls for Washington to be accommodated.
North Korea's Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho has accused the U.S. government at the U.N.-general debate of blocking negotiations for a nuclear-weapon-free Korean peninsula with its continuing sanctions.
"The recent stalenge comes because the U.S. builds on coercive measures that are fatal for confidence-building," Ri said in New York.
Disarmament is unthinkable for North Korea in these circumstances.
"Without any trust in the U.S., there will be no confidence in our national security and in such circumstances there will be no way that we are the first to disarm ourselves," he said.
That punitive measures could force North Korea to the "knees" is "a wishful dream of people who don't know us."
In order to build trust, however, his country has stopped its nuclear and missile tests and destroyed a nuclear test facility, Ri emphasized.
However, there have been no "corresponding reactions" from the U.S. side, criticized the minister.
Due to North Korea’s nuclear and missile program, the U.N. U.N. Security Council had decided three times last year on sanctions against the internationally isolated country.
U.S. President Donald Trump and his Secretary Of State Mike Pompeo have been pushingly complying with punitive measures over the past few days.
At the same time, the U.S. government is working on a rapprochement.
In June, Trump met North Korea's leader Kim Jong Un for the first time.
After the first summit in Singapore, the U.S. president is aiming for a second summit with Kim.
In October, Pompeo is due to travel to Pyongyang again for talks about ending North Korea's nuclear weapons programme.
The HoT serves: New cuisine with donations from the community
The fact that they were actually in a youth meeting, the many visitors to the open-door HoT house did not notice: white blankets and floral decorations on the tables and a large buffet waited for the guests after the Thanksgiving service.
The young people of the HoT had conjured the buffet with delicacies from different countries in the new kitchen.
The institution was financed by donations from the community, for which the young people wanted to thank the food.
The start with the new facility was not exactly easy, as Henriette Westbuer, the head of the HoT, remembers.
We had already filled our refrigerators with food and ingredients before the summer break and were well prepared for the big day.
But then on the first day after the summer break came the nasty surprise: a power failure had put the huge, two-part refrigerator out of service, the food was spoiled and had to be disposed of.
"Now we had to start all over again: Although we got a lot of support from friends and neighbors, but the children and young people wanted to make it alone," says Westbuer.
And that's how everyone took part.
