Moorbrand in Meppen: Still almost 1000 emergency personnel in use.
Around four weeks after the eruption of the moor fire on a Bundeswehr site in Emsland, 980 emergency personnel are still engaged in the firefighting work.
This was announced by the Bundeswehr on Sunday.
According to a spokesperson, the situation continues to develop stably, 70 volunteers have recently been withdrawn.
A further reduction in the number of emergency forces is planned, it says.
In the night of Sunday, an above-ground fire had been discovered and extinguished.
Last week, the disaster had been lifted, which was proclaimed a good week earlier on 21 September, because it had been feared that that the flames would put adjacent forests and settlements at risk due to wind and spark flight.
On Thursday, it was then said that a spread of the fire to adjoining communities no longer threatens.
At times, more than 1500 emergency personnel tried to get the major fire under control.
The Luftwaffe continues to support the work on the Bundeswehr test site near Meppen with a transport helicopter.
There are also reconnaissance flights with drones of the Bundeswehr.
According to the Bundeswehr, reconnaissance flights with "tornadoes" are also planned again for October 2 and 5.
The blaze had been sparked by test missiles that Airbus technicians had fired.
The smoke plume of the moor fire had at times moved up to a hundred kilometers over the northwest of Germany to Bremen.
On Monday, the Bundeswehr wants to set up a point of contact, which accepts damage reports.
