Switzerland launches electronic register for organ donors
The register is intended to facilitate organ donations and relieve relatives in stressful situations.
The new procedure does not change the fact that organs may still only be withdrawn with explicit consent, emphasizes Swisstransplant.
(dpa) Switzerland is launching an electronic register for organ donors this Monday.
This should facilitate organ donations and relieve relatives in stressful situations.
People who want to provide their organs in the event of death can register there.
Your wishes are then available from Swisstransplant if necessary.
The new procedure does not change the fact that organs – as in Germany – can only be removed with explicit consent, emphasizes Swisstransplant.
It is assumed that 15 percent of the Swiss have a donor card, says Franz Immer, director of the Swisstransplant Foundation.
But only in five percent of cases, a card is found at the decisive moment with possible donors.
In more than 60 percent of the conversations, the relatives do not know the wish of the deceased.
"You then have to decide representatively in the sense of the deceased, which is often very burdensome," says Immer.
A similar registration system already exists in the UK.
In Germany, there are considerations to store information about the possible organ donation on the health insurance card, as the spokeswoman of the Federal Center for Health Education, Marita Völker-Albert says.
"There's not a register in which people can register their desires - pro or versus organ donation - doesn't exist," she says.
Federal Health Minister Jens Spahn has advocated a new regulation of organ donation.
According to his proposal, everyone would initially be considered organ donors, unless he or relatives disagree.
Among other things, the Catholic Church rejects this.
14-year-old pupil is world champion in heading
14-year-old student Tomohiro Iseda from Japan is a world champion in head computing.
He prevailed over the weekend in Wolfsburg against 39 competitors.
This was announced by Martina Flamme-Jasper from the Phaeno Science Museum, the venue of the competition, on Sunday.
The 16-year-old Wenzel Grüß from Germany took third place.
The youngest World Cup participant was a twelve-year-old Japanese pupil, the oldest a 74-year-old pensioner from France, as chief referee Ralf Laue said.
The computer science professor from Zwickau (Saxony) organizes the competition every two years.
Previous tasks were, for example: 14,130 214 x 17,981.822 or 57,809,355 + 28 x 16,448,333.
Since spectators were not allowed, there was a show on Saturday afternoon where participants competed against guests from the audience with calculator.
Wenzel Grüß from Lastrup demonstrated how he holds a ball with his head in his air when calculating.
He is one of seven Germans in the competition.
In total, 82 candidates from more than 20 countries attempted to qualify for the competition.
Grüß was already present two years ago as a 14-year-old in Bielefeld.
With terms such as superbrain, genius or prodigy, organizer Laue can do little.
"Head calculation can be trained," he said.
Many participants practiced several hours a day.
In India or Singapore, there are even stadium-oriented numeracy competitions.
Disciplines include, for example, square root drawing from six-digit numbers.
When calculating versatilities, the candidates get surprise tasks, for which they have to quickly find a solution strategy.
The Germans would also have good chances here, the main referee said.
Otherwise, Asians are often at an advantage, because in the early affiliaties, head calculations are learned more intensively and with other, effective methods.
