Farmers are allowed to begin annual kinking care on Monday
Schleswig-Holstein's farmers are allowed to start annual kink care on Monday.
The "green ramparts", which are unique in Germany, have shaped the landscape of the forest-poor state for around 200 years.
The up to five-meter-wide tree and scrubbery are "forest substitutes" and for many animals and plants the last retreats.
Knicks connect scattered habitats as corridors, through which the exchange of organisms can still work, as the managing director of the Naturschutzbund Deutschland (NABU) in Schleswig-Holstein, Ingo Ludwichowski, said.
Knicks are living fences
The knicks were created on the basis of an order of the Dane King Christian VI in 1766, which ruled over Schleswig-Holstein.
The "living fences" were not only field boundary and cattle fence, they also provided the farmers with firewood and beams for their half-timbered houses.
His name "Knick" also comes from that time: by "buckling" the branches, the dense, intertwined demarcations could develop.
Today, around 45 000 kilometres of the hedges extend between the North Sea and the Baltic Sea and give the image of a wood-rich, diversely structured landscape.
According to NABU, they are now used by around 7000 animal species as a substitute habitat.
Up to 1800 different species could live in a kilometre of kink and breed up to 30 pairs of birds.
However, the kink can only fulfil its ecologically important function if it is cultivated appropriately in winter.
Otherwise, he stumbles to a light row of trees that, in addition to biodiversity, also loses the wind protection effect, Ludwichowski said.
According to the Ministry of the Ministry of Agriculture, kink care may only be operated outside the breeding season from October to March.
After Tsunami on Sulawesi Indonesier talks about situation on the ground
In an interview with the editorial network Germany (RND), the local Radika Pinto describes the situation on site on the island of Sulawesi after the earthquakes and the tsunami:
Were you there during the tsunami itself?
No, I was just in Jakarta on the island of Java at that time.
When the earthquakes were, my wife called me, she was in Palu.
When I found out about the tsunami, I immediately made my way back to Sulawesi.
That's when I arrived on Sunday morning because I still had to drive more than 20 hours after a flight because the airport on Palu was closed at the time.
What's the situation on the island now?
The national government has reopened the airport so that relief supplies can also be flowned in.
When I arrived I saw the houses on the coast, which are all completely destroyed.
Also, our "World Vision" office is destroyed, but we've provisionally set up something where we've accommodated other people as well.
Where are the people living on Sulawesi now?
Many have run onto surrounding hills where they are still waiting for fear of another tsunami and earthquake and where they don't have access to water and food.
Some are staying in front of their homes, there are still aftershocks.
Because many grocery stores and also the gas stations are completely closed, people partly steal food and gasoline.
The public service is completely shut down, the national government is helping out.
A prison has also been destroyed, the detainees partly roam freely and the search for them is difficult.
There are still many bodies lying around, many people have not yet been founded and are missing.
How is the communication and information supply on the island?
At the moment we have mobile phone reception, but before that didn't work and we only got information about the radio.
Many people are still looking for their children, neighbors and other people who they don't know whether they're living and where they aren't going at work during the Earthquakes.
The tsunami warning system didn't work properly.
Do you have information on this?
We don't know the reasons for this, but when the all-clear came, many thought "Okay, no tsunami" - and then he came.
In other regions that have not been hit by the tsunami, there has been the misinformation that a tsunami encounters the region and they have run to the surrounding mountains.
Isn't an island like Sulawesi, which lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire, prepared for earthquakes?
Yes, there are earthquakes every day, but this was the worst.
We usually know how to react to earthquakes and also train evacuations.
But the complete infrastructure has collapsed, the earthquake has hit a lot of people.
