Prof. Dr. Aladin El-Mafaalani discusses integration: A discussion on an equal footing
In front of them is the author, Professor Doctor Aladin El-Mafaalani.
He once again accepted the invitation of Ernst Reiling and Lore Hauschild.
"For many years, I have made it my task to bring interesting people to Greven," explains the founder and chairman of the "Reckenfelder Treff" - the association that cooperates with the "Cultural Policy Alliance" in hosting the evening.
El-Mafalani enters the reading very loosely, describes the writing process of the book, talks about how difficult it was to explain the topic in a comprehensible way.
With a lot of gestures, tangible numbers and one or the other wit, he brings listeners closer to one of the most conflicting topics: migration and integration.
Why arise increasing conflicts despite successful integration?
The Syrian-born author uses a metaphor when answering the question.
German society sits at a table, the migrants of the first generation at the cat table.
Their children grow up in Germany and want to go to the table.
The third generation of migrants was born in Germany, they have always sat at the big table and now want to participate.
This leads to conflict.
It is therefore not decisive whether one classifies politically right or left, but: "Am I for or against an open society?", he emphasizes.
Initially rather subdued, then the discussion round starts more and more vigorously.
And there is need for speech.
An Afghan refugee reports from his perspective, two teachers tell how they see successful or unsuccessful integration among pupils.
El-Mafaalani stands in front, not on stage, a symbol for the discussion: she is on an equal footing.
The sociology professor does not urge his opinion, emphasizes that he speaks of the average, not of individual fates.
He is pleased that there is interest in the issue, because "only when we understand what the problem is, we can find solutions," says El-Mafaalani.
Even during the ensuing signing hour, he has an open ear for the questions of the guests and also in the foyer was intensively discussed further, with prepared Kurdish specialties.
